const rcLinked=rc.filter(x=>x.link_note&&!isW(x)).map(rcLab);$execution.customData.set('rc_count',String(rc.length));$execution.customData.set('rc_linked_count',String(rcLinked.length));
const rcSection=rc.length>0?"HELD CONCLUSIONS (LIVE RECORD -- your private record: build on these, never cite them by number or label in the debate; state what each holds and quote its verses):\n"+rc.map(x=>rcLab(x)+(x.link_note?' '+String(x.link_note):'')+' ['+x.status+', '+x.layer+'] ('+(x.passage_anchor||'')+'): '+(rcLab(x).indexOf('S-')===0&&!isW(x)?String(x.conclusion_text||'').slice(0,300):String(x.conclusion_text||''))).join('\n')+"\nRule: where a retrieved conclusion covers the ground, build on it: state its finding in your own words and quote the verses it stands on. Re-deriving settled ground from scratch is drift. A conclusion's STATUS is part of the record -- SUBSTANTIALLY_SUPPORTED is not CONFIRMED; do not inflate. If retrieval returns nothing relevant, say so and proceed from the text."+(rcLinked.length>0?"\nA row marked LINKED was not found by the topic search; it is served because an approved corpus link ties it to a retrieved conclusion, and the note names the link. Weigh it like any held conclusion, at its own status and layer.":"")+(rc.some(isSL)?"\nA row marked SIGHT LINK is served whole because an approved sight link group ties it to this topic's words; the note names the link. Weigh it at its own status and layer.":"")+(rc.some(isG)?"\nA row marked GUARD SET is served whole because the record ties it to the named drift guard for topics like this one: it is the record's own answer on that guard's ground. State its finding and quote its verses, at its own status and layer.":""):"HELD CONCLUSIONS (LIVE RECORD): retrieval returned nothing this run. Do NOT cite conclusion numbers -- none are in front of you; citing a number from memory is fabrication and drift. State plainly that no held conclusions were retrieved this run and proceed from the KJV text alone.";
const LAYER="LAYER RULES:\n[L1]=KJV text\n[L2]=Hebrew/Greek\n[L3]=cross-reference\n[L4]=inference";
const DEF_BURDEN="\n\nBURDEN RULE (scored by the judge): you are the DEFENDER this debate. Tag every load-bearing claim with its layer ([L1] KJV text, [L2] Hebrew/Greek, [L3] cross-reference, [L4] inference). LAYER-ADMISSIBILITY (Isaiah 28:10 -- the framework judges with the framework): No load-bearing inference may float free of the text. An [L4] counts as evidence only if it rests on -- and exhibits -- AT LEAST TWO prior layers of footing: a stating [L1] verse, an [L2] lexical ground, or an [L3] cross-reference. It must show the chain from each exhibited footing to the inference (precept upon precept, line upon line). Not all three registers are required; which two the claim leans on is the claim's own, but two is the floor. An [L4] that exhibits fewer than two prior layers floats free: it is inadmissible, carries zero weight, and your opponent will name it. The challenger will close with an UNSUPPORTED-L4 PREMISES list; you will then face a LINK ROUND -- for each listed premise, either LINK n: SUPPORTED -- exhibit its layer footing (the [L1]/[L2]/[L3] anchors it rests on and the chain to it), or LINK n: CONCEDED -- it floats free. A link honestly conceded as floating is DISCIPLINE, not defeat; a link whose footing you EXHIBIT stands as evidence. In your LINK ROUND, quote every verse the challenger pressed against a link. A press must have a premise behind it; a verse pressed without one is not a witness (operator ruling 392): only a verse carried under PRESSED WITH: beside a numbered premise of a side's list is a pressed witness, and a verse on a side's 'cited in' line, or pressed in prose against a link but under no listed premise, is not a witness and is never counted unanswered. A judge who grades a link BROKEN because the witnesses exclude, deny or establish something must quote the words that state it; where the quoted words, read in their own context and with the other verses the judge quotes, do not state it, the ground is the judge's own [L4] inference, and it counts only when the judge exhibits two lower layers ([L1]-[L3]) for it, as submitted evidence must; otherwise it is no ground for BROKEN, and the link is graded under the remaining rules. Everything presented is open to challenge, and what the other side never challenges stands as held for the side that presented it. VERSE CITATION (binding, operator ruling 3): cite every verse by book, chapter and verse (for example Matthew 1:25) and quote its words exactly as the KJV gives them; a quotation may be part of a verse. A verse alluded to, paraphrased or misquoted, or named without its book, chapter and verse, is not counted as evidence or as an answer, and the system checks every quotation against the KJV text. After the link rounds you have a CLOSE ROUND: your final defense, in which you answer every witness still pressed against your chain.";
const CH_BURDEN="Tag every substantive claim with its layer marker: [L1]=KJV text, [L2]=Hebrew/Greek, [L3]=cross-reference, [L4]=inference. Untagged claims are inadmissible. BURDEN RULE (scored by the judge -- it binds you exactly as it binds the defender): tag every load-bearing objection with its layer. DG-61 binds your own case: no verse is required to state a conclusion, for either side. Argue from the rows that affirm your reading; never rest a premise on the demand that a verse state an exclusion or a denial in its own words, and never charge the defender with lacking such a verse. LAYER-ADMISSIBILITY (Isaiah 28:10 -- the framework judges with the framework): No load-bearing inference may float free of the text. An [L4] counts as evidence only if it rests on -- and exhibits -- AT LEAST TWO prior layers of footing: a stating [L1] verse, an [L2] lexical ground, or an [L3] cross-reference. It must show the chain from each exhibited footing to the inference (precept upon precept, line upon line). Not all three registers are required; which two the claim leans on is the claim's own, but two is the floor. An [L4] that exhibits fewer than two prior layers floats free: it is inadmissible, carries zero weight, and your opponent will name it. The defender's final round will close with a list of YOUR unsupported [L4] premises; you will then face your own LINK ROUND -- exhibit the layer footing or concede. An objection that is itself a floating [L4] cannot break a link that exhibits its footing. In your LINK ROUND, quote every verse the defender pressed against your chain. A press must have a premise behind it; a verse pressed without one is not a witness (operator ruling 392): only a verse carried under PRESSED WITH: beside a numbered premise of a side's list is a pressed witness, and a verse on a side's 'cited in' line, or pressed in prose against a link but under no listed premise, is not a witness and is never counted unanswered. A judge who grades a link BROKEN because the witnesses exclude, deny or establish something must quote the words that state it; where the quoted words, read in their own context and with the other verses the judge quotes, do not state it, the ground is the judge's own [L4] inference, and it counts only when the judge exhibits two lower layers ([L1]-[L3]) for it, as submitted evidence must; otherwise it is no ground for BROKEN, and the link is graded under the remaining rules. Everything presented is open to challenge, and what the other side never challenges stands as held for the side that presented it. VERSE CITATION (binding, operator ruling 3): cite every verse by book, chapter and verse (for example Matthew 1:25) and quote its words exactly as the KJV gives them; a quotation may be part of a verse. A verse alluded to, paraphrased or misquoted, or named without its book, chapter and verse, is not counted as evidence or as an answer, and the system checks every quotation against the KJV text. After the link rounds you have a CLOSE ROUND: your final words on your own position, in which you may not press any new witness or premise.";
const TEXTCORR="The text corrects the commentator -- always. That rule governs [L4] inference against [L1] text; NEVER aim it at scripture itself, including an apostle's inspired application of an earlier text. Scriptures interlock (Isaiah 28:10) -- they do not compete, and setting verse against verse is itself drift.";
